[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Equivalences","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Equivalences"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internals","page":"API reference","text":"These functions are not exported. They implement the quadratic relation used by SugarGravityQuad.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#UnitfulBrewing","page":"API reference","text":"UnitfulBrewing\n\nModule extending Unitful.jl with beer brewing units, and with equivalences, via UnitfulEquivalences.jl, between quantities that are used interchangeably in brewing.\n\nThe units are available through Unitful's u\"...\" string macro once the package is loaded, e.g. u\"°P\", u\"SRM\" or u\"vols\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"UnitfulBrewing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#UnitfulBrewing.SugarGravity","page":"API reference","text":"SugarGravity()\n\nEquivalence to convert between Sugar Contents and Specific Gravity quantities.\n\nConvert between degrees Plato and specific gravity according to the equation\n\nPlato = 259 * (1 - 1/sg),\n\nExamples\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"°P\", 1.040u\"sg\", SugarGravity())\n9.961538461538483 °P\njulia> uconvert(u\"sg\", 15u\"°P\", SugarGravity())\n1.0614754098360655 sg\njulia> uconvert(u\"gu\", 12u\"°P\", SugarGravity())\n48.58299595141693 gu\njulia> uconvert(u\"°P\", 40u\"gu\", SugarGravity())\n9.961538461538483 °P\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"UnitfulBrewing.SugarGravity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#UnitfulBrewing.SugarGravityQuad","page":"API reference","text":"SugarGravityQuad()\n\nEquivalence to convert between Sugar Contents and Specific Gravity quantities.\n\nConvert between degrees Plato and gravity units according to the quadratic equation\n\nPlato = 0.25802gu - 0.00020535gu^2,\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula for specific gravity\n\nPlato = 668.72 * sg - 463.37 - 205.35 * sg^2,\n\nwith\n\ngu = 1000 ( sg - 1.000 ).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"°P\", 1.040u\"sg\", SugarGravityQuad())\n9.992240000000002 °P\njulia> uconvert(u\"sg\", 15u\"°P\", SugarGravityQuad())\n1.0611068377146748 sg\njulia> uconvert(u\"gu\", 12u\"°P\", SugarGravityQuad())\n48.370088784473296 gu\njulia> uconvert(u\"°P\", 40u\"gu\", SugarGravityQuad())\n9.992240000000002 °P\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"UnitfulBrewing.SugarGravityQuad"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#UnitfulBrewing.DensityConcentration","page":"API reference","text":"DensityConcentration()\n\nEquivalence to convert between Density and Concentration for water-based solutions, with relatively small quantities of solutes, as in water treatment, so that 1 mg/L is equivalent to 1 ppm (parts per million).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"mg/l\", 10u\"ppm\", DensityConcentration())\n10 mg L⁻¹\njulia> uconvert(u\"ppm\", 1u\"g/l\", DensityConcentration())\n1000.0 ppm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"UnitfulBrewing.DensityConcentration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#UnitfulBrewing.CarbonationDensity","page":"API reference","text":"CarbonationDensity()\n\nEquivalence to convert between carbonation, in volumes of CO₂ per volume of beer (vols), and the density of dissolved CO₂, using the density of CO₂ at 0 °C and 1 atm, so that 1 vols is equivalent to 1.977 g/L.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"g/l\", 2.5u\"vols\", CarbonationDensity())\n4.9425 g L⁻¹\njulia> uconvert(u\"vols\", 5u\"g/l\", CarbonationDensity())\n2.529084471421345 vols\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"UnitfulBrewing.CarbonationDensity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#UnitfulBrewing.gu_to_plato","page":"API reference","text":"gu_to_plato(gu::Number)\n\nConvert a value in gravity units to degrees Plato according to the quadratic formula\n\nPlato = 0.25802 gu - 0.00020535 gu^2,\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula for specific gravity\n\nPlato = 668.72 sg - 463.37 - 205.35 sg^2,\n\nwith gu = 1000 (sg - 1).\n\nThis is the relation used by SugarGravityQuad.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"UnitfulBrewing.gu_to_plato"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#UnitfulBrewing.plato_to_gu","page":"API reference","text":"plato_to_gu(p::Number)\n\nConvert a value in degrees Plato to gravity units by inverting the quadratic formula of gu_to_plato, taking the root in the range of interest:\n\ngu = e - sqrt(e^2 - g p),\n\nwhere\n\ne = 0.25802 / (2 × 0.00020535) = 628.2444606768931,\ng = 1 / 0.00020535 = 4869.734599464329.\n\nFor p > e^2 / g ≈ 81.05, far beyond any wort, the quadratic has no real inverse, and e is returned just for definiteness, although the conversion is meaningless there.\n\nThis is the relation used by SugarGravityQuad.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"UnitfulBrewing.plato_to_gu"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Dimensions-and-units","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"","title":"Dimensions and units"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Dimensions","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"UnitfulBrewing adds the following dimensions to the ones in Unitful:\n\nDimension Quantity Reference unit\n𝐒𝐂 Sugar contents °P\n𝐂𝐋 Color SRM\n𝐁𝐓 Bitterness IBU\n𝐃𝐏 Diastatic power °Lintner\n𝐂𝐁 Carbonation vols\n\nEach one is a dimension of its own, so, for instance, a color cannot be accidentally added to a bitterness or converted to a plain number.","title":"Dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Sugar-contents-and-gravity","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\n°P degree Plato reference unit for 𝐒𝐂\nBrix degree Brix currently equal to °P\nBalling degree Balling currently equal to °P\nsg specific gravity dimensionless, 1 sg = 1\ngu gravity unit affine: gu = 1000 (sg - 1)\ngp gravity point alias of gu\n\nA specific gravity of 1.040 is 40 gravity units:\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"gu\", 1.040u\"sg\")\n40.0 gu\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"sg\", 48u\"gu\")\n1.048 sg\n\nSugar contents and specific gravity have different dimensions, so converting between them requires one of the equivalences SugarGravity or SugarGravityQuad; see Equivalences.","title":"Sugar contents and gravity"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Color","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\nSRM Standard Reference Method reference unit for 𝐂𝐋\nEBC European Brewery Convention EBC = 1.97 SRM\n°L degree Lovibond affine: SRM = 1.3546 °L - 0.76\n\nsrm, ebc, Lovi and Lovibond are aliases of SRM, EBC and °L.\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"EBC\", 10u\"SRM\")\n197//10 EBC\n\njulia> round(u\"°L\", 20u\"SRM\", digits=2)\n15.33 °L","title":"Color"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Bitterness","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\nIBU International Bitterness Unit reference unit for 𝐁𝐓","title":"Bitterness"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Diastatic-power","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\n°Lintner degree Lintner reference unit for 𝐃𝐏\n°WK Windisch–Kolbach unit affine: °WK = 3.5 °Lintner - 16\n\nLintner and WK are aliases of °Lintner and °WK.\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"°Lintner\", 19u\"°WK\")\n10//1 °Lintner","title":"Diastatic power"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Carbonation","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\nvols volumes of CO₂ reference unit for 𝐂𝐁: volumes of CO₂, at 0 °C and 1 atm, dissolved per volume of beer\n\nvols is the unit used for carbonation by BeerJSON. Use the equivalence CarbonationDensity to convert to and from the mass of CO₂ per volume, in g/l.","title":"Carbonation"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Concentration","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unitful (since v1.19) already defines percent, permille, pertenthousand, pcm, ppm, ppb, ppt and ppq, which are used here as they are. Use the equivalence DensityConcentration to convert between these and mass per volume, such as mg/l.","title":"Concentration"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#pH","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\npH⁺ power of hydrogen logarithmic unit, base 10\npwrH power of hydrogen alias of pH⁺\n\nThe usual symbol pH cannot be used, since Unitful already defines pH as the picohenry, a unit of electrical inductance.\n\njulia> 3u\"pH⁺\" < 5u\"pwrH\"\ntrue","title":"pH"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Time","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"These are aliases for Unitful's time units, under the names used by BeerJSON:\n\nAlias Unitful unit\nsec s\nmin minute\nday d\nweek wk\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"min\", 1u\"hr\")\n60 minute","title":"Time"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Volumes","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"","title":"Volumes"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#US-volumes","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\ngal US gallon 231 in³ (≈ 3.785 L)\nqt US quart gal/4\npt US pint qt/2\nfloz US fluid ounce pt/16\nbbl US beer barrel 31 gal (≈ 117.35 L)\n\nNote that bbl is the US beer barrel, of 31 US gallons, and not the 42-gallon oil barrel.","title":"US volumes"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Imperial-volumes","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\nifloz imperial fluid ounce 28.4130625 mL\ngi gill 5 ifloz\nipt imperial pint 20 ifloz\niqt imperial quart 2 ipt\nigal imperial gallon 8 ipt (= 4.54609 L)\nibbl imperial beer barrel 36 igal","title":"Imperial volumes"},{"category":"section","location":"units/#Culinary-volumes","page":"Dimensions and units","text":"Unit Name Definition\ncup cup pt/2\ntbsp tablespoon cup/16\ntsp teaspoon tbsp/3\n\njulia> round(u\"l\", 1u\"bbl\", digits=2)\n117.35 L\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"ipt\", 1u\"igal\")\n8 ipt\n\njulia> round(u\"l\", 1u\"igal\", digits=5)\n4.54609 L\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"floz\", 1u\"cup\")\n8.0 floz","title":"Culinary volumes"},{"category":"section","location":"equivalences/#Equivalences","page":"Equivalences","text":"Some quantities used in brewing measure different things but are commonly treated as interchangeable, according to well-established relations. Degrees Plato and specific gravity are the typical example: the first is a mass fraction of sugar and the second a ratio of densities, but both estimate the amount of fermentables in the wort.\n\nSince these quantities have different dimensions, Unitful does not convert between them directly. Instead, UnitfulBrewing defines equivalences, using UnitfulEquivalences.jl. An equivalence is passed as the last argument of uconvert:\n\nuconvert(target_unit, quantity, equivalence)","title":"Equivalences"},{"category":"section","location":"equivalences/#Sugar-contents-and-gravity","page":"Equivalences","text":"Two equivalences relate degrees Plato (dimension 𝐒𝐂) to specific gravity and gravity units.\n\nSugarGravity uses the rational relation\n\ntextPlato = 259left(1 - frac1textsgright)\n\nSugarGravityQuad uses the quadratic relation\n\ntextPlato = 66872textsg - 46337 - 20535textsg^2\n\nwhich, in gravity units textgu = 1000(textsg - 1), reads\n\ntextPlato = 025802textgu - 000020535textgu^2\n\nBoth work in either direction and with any of the units °P, Brix, Balling, sg and gu:\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"°P\", 1.040u\"sg\", SugarGravity())\n9.961538461538483 °P\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"sg\", 10u\"°P\", SugarGravity())\n1.0401606425702812 sg\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"gu\", 10u\"°P\", SugarGravityQuad())\n40.032121145872225 gu\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"°P\", 1.048u\"sg\", SugarGravityQuad())\n11.911833600000001 °P\n\nIn the range of interest for brewing, from 0 °P to about 30 °P, the two equivalences differ by less than 0.4%.","title":"Sugar contents and gravity"},{"category":"section","location":"equivalences/#Density-and-concentration","page":"Equivalences","text":"For water-based solutions with small amounts of solutes, as in brewing water treatment, a concentration of 1 ppm (by mass) is treated as equivalent to 1 mg/l. DensityConcentration implements this, relating any dimensionless concentration (ppm, ppb, percent, …) to a mass per volume:\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"mg/l\", 10u\"ppm\", DensityConcentration())\n10 mg L⁻¹\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"ppm\", 1u\"g/l\", DensityConcentration())\n1000.0 ppm","title":"Density and concentration"},{"category":"section","location":"equivalences/#Carbonation-and-density","page":"Equivalences","text":"Carbonation is usually given in volumes of CO₂ per volume of beer (vols), but sometimes as the mass of dissolved CO₂ per volume, in g/l. CarbonationDensity relates these using the density of CO₂ at 0 °C and 1 atm, so that 1 vols is equivalent to 1.977 g/l:\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"g/l\", 2.5u\"vols\", CarbonationDensity())\n4.9425 g L⁻¹\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"vols\", 5u\"g/l\", CarbonationDensity())\n2.529084471421345 vols","title":"Carbonation and density"},{"category":"section","location":"equivalences/#Not-yet-implemented","page":"Equivalences","text":"An equivalence between bitterness in IBU and the concentration of iso-alpha acids in mg/l, with 1 IBU equivalent to 1 mg/l.\nVolumes of gases other than CO₂, such as N₂ for nitro beers.","title":"Not yet implemented"},{"category":"section","location":"#UnitfulBrewing.jl","page":"Home","text":"A supplemental units package for Unitful.jl, with the units used in beer brewing: sugar content and gravity, color, bitterness, diastatic power, carbonation, pH, and US, imperial and culinary volumes.\n\nIt also defines equivalences, via UnitfulEquivalences.jl, for converting between quantities that measure different things but are commonly used interchangeably in brewing, such as degrees Plato and specific gravity.\n\nnote: Note\nThis package is still under development and not yet registered.","title":"UnitfulBrewing.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Since the package is not registered yet, install it from its repository:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaBrewing/UnitfulBrewing.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Load Unitful together with UnitfulBrewing. The brewing units are then available through Unitful's u\"...\" string macro, like any other unit:\n\njulia> using Unitful, UnitfulBrewing\n\njulia> float(uconvert(u\"l\", 5u\"gal\"))\n18.92705892 L\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"EBC\", 10u\"SRM\")\n197//10 EBC\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"sg\", 12u\"°P\", SugarGravity())\n1.048582995951417 sg\n\njulia> uconvert(u\"g/l\", 2.5u\"vols\", CarbonationDensity())\n4.9425 g L⁻¹","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Dimensions and units lists the dimensions and units this package defines.\nEquivalences describes the conversions between related quantities, with the formulas they use.\nAPI reference documents the exported equivalence types and the internal conversion functions.","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"Home","text":"This package is licensed under the MIT license.","title":"License"}]
